prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a 3D maze game with automatic pathfinding. The player explores a procedurally generated maze from a first-person perspective and searches for the exit.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { PointerLockControls } from 'three/addons/controls/PointerLockControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Maze Generation]
(1) Procedurally generate a 15×15 maze grid using DFS (recursive backtracking) or Prim's algorithm;
(2) Each cell must be 2×2 units (that is, the maze must have a total footprint of 30×30 units);
(3) Use BoxGeometry for the walls (width 0.2, height 3, with depth depending on orientation), with a dark MeshStandardMaterial;
(4) Use a light-colored material for passage floors and a dark-colored material for floors at wall positions to create visual distinction;

[Markers]
(5) Place a green marker pillar at the start point (0, 0) using CylinderGeometry with an emissive green appearance;
(6) Place a red marker pillar at the exit (14, 14);

[First-Person Controls]
(7) Use PointerLockControls to implement mouse-look controls;
(8) Use WASD for movement at a speed of 5 units/s;
(9) Collision detection: the player must not be able to pass through maze walls. Cast a Raycaster in the movement direction (with a distance of approximately 0.6); if it hits a wall, prevent movement in that direction;

[Minimap]
(10) Place a 200×200-pixel 2D minimap in the upper-right corner (id="minimap", using a <canvas> element);
(11) The minimap must show a top-down view of the entire maze layout (walls = black lines, passages = white);
(12) Display the player's position on the minimap in real time as a blue dot;
(13) Mark the exit on the minimap with a red dot;

[Pathfinding]
(14) "Solve" button (id="solveBtn"): when clicked, use BFS or the A* algorithm to calculate the shortest path from the player's current position to the exit;
(15) Display the path in the 3D scene using a colored line (Line or TubeGeometry);
(16) After solving, set solved=true, and record the path length (number of cells) in pathLength;

[Regeneration]
(17) "New Maze" button (id="newMazeBtn"): when clicked, regenerate the maze, return the player to the starting point, and clear the path;

[Timer]
(18) Timer (id="timer"): begin timing when the player moves for the first time, stop when the player reaches the exit, and display it in "MM:SS" format;

[HUD Display]
(19) Display the following in the interface:
  - Timer (id="timer")
  - Solve button (id="solveBtn")
  - New Maze button (id="newMazeBtn")
  - Minimap canvas (id="minimap")

[State Exposure]
(20) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame with the following fields:
  - mazeSize: number (15)
  - playerCell: { row, col } (coordinates of the maze cell currently occupied by the player)
  - exitCell: { row, col } (exit cell coordinates, fixed at {row:14, col:14})
  - solved: boolean (whether the path has been solved)
  - pathLength: number (length of the solved path; 0 when unsolved)
  - timer: number (elapsed time in seconds)
  - wallCount: number (total number of wall segments in the maze)

Output a single HTML file that can run by being opened directly in a browser.
